  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM MDT Thu May 21 2026\n\nThe 21/12Z H500 hand analysis map shows an pseudo-rex pattern in\nthe center of NOAM...involving a trough extending from the\nnorthern High Plains toward the Central Rockies and a short wave\nridge moving over the northern Canadian Prairies. At 18Z the\ncold front associated with this trough was dropping through\nWyoming toward northern Utah and should dip into our northern\nCWA over the next few hours. General forcing with the trough is\nleading convective light showers/virga across our northern CWA \nattm with any measurable precipitation over the high Uintas so \nfar. The stronger/more organized convection is firing along\nbehind the fropa and as such we should see an uptick in\nthunderstorm chances across the northern tier of counties\nthrough the afternoon. Gusty outflow winds will be the main\nthreat with the virga already producing a few gusts near 45 mph.  \nShowers linger over the northern mountains through midnight \nwith a little help from orographic forcing with quiet predawn\nhours expected. Colder air filtering in behind this front and \nsome clearing looks to drop temperatures into the upper 20s \nagain over the upper Yampa and White River Valleys and another \nFreeze Warning is in place there for tonight. The front will \ncontinue to make progress southward overnight and looks to \nsettle near or slightly north of our southern border by sunrise \non Friday. A trailing wave and curved jet structure aloft looks \nto provide enough forcing to release instability near and north \nof the front by tomorrow morning. This convective activity looks\nto favor the southern mountains but there could be some very \nhigh based showers that drift in from central Utah on the nose \nof another jet couplet. Temperatures cool slightly behind the \nfront and trough for tomorrow with highs a few degrees cooler \nthan normal in the south to 10 to 15 cooler across the far \nnorth. Hence there remains a small threat of freezing again \nSaturday morning but confidence not there yet for headlines. The\ntrough will move farther onto the Plains by Saturday while high\npressure bulges in from the South. This will push temperatures \nback to near and above for the weekend into the Monday Holiday. \nThere seems to be just enough instability and mositure for\nisolated to widely scattered mountain storm through the weekend.\nBy Monday a southern stream system arriving to SoCal increases\nsoutherly flow into the 4 Corners...advecting additional\nmoisture with an uptick in convective coverage for Memorial Day.\nLooks like an active an cooler end the forecast if the EURO\nverifies as the SoCal system is forced across the 4 Corners by a\na much stronger system digging into the West. The GFS is much\nless amplified by the middle of next week however it appears the\nensemble clusters may be leaning away from that attm. There \nwill be a lot of wiggle room in the forecast depending on how \nthis plays out.",
